      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">1. Fluid Micro-Cavitation &amp; Net Positive Suction Head (NPSH)</h4>
          <p>
            When strong acids contact calcium carbonate, rapid effervescence releases high volumes of dissolved carbon dioxide gas (<em class="font-mono">CaCO₃ + 2H⁺ → Ca²⁺ + H₂O + CO₂↑</em>). Inside the wash pump volute, localized pressure drops below liquid vapor pressure, producing millions of microscopic vapor cavities. As these bubbles sweep toward high-pressure impeller vanes, they collapse violently with micro-jet velocities exceeding 800 m/s and acoustic pressures over 1,000 MPa, pitting 304 stainless steel impellers and shattering brittle mechanical seal faces.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">2. Antoine Acid Vaporization &amp; OSHA Inhalation Toxicology</h4>
          <p>
            The volatility of industrial descaling formulations follows the Antoine vapor pressure relationship (<em class="font-mono">log₁₀ P = A - B/(T+C)</em>). While pure phosphoric acid has relatively low volatility at 20°C, operating a spray wash manifold at 65°C–80°C shears the liquid into ultrafine sub-micron aerosol droplets (<em class="font-mono">d &lt; 2.5 µm</em>). In unventilated dishrooms, these inhalable acid mists rapidly breach the OSHA Permissible Exposure Limit (PEL) of 1.0 mg/m³, penetrating deep alveolar pathways and inducing acute bronchospasm and laryngotracheal ulceration.
          </p>
